hi

If the sole objective is to prevent a specific consumer from reading
records, consider utilizing quotas to limit read throughput based on the
client ID. While this approach may not provide partition-level control, it
effectively allows administrators to regulate data flow for individual
consumers.

> Also, would it make more sense to just think of freezing partitions
themselves to be consumed by any listeners instead of focusing on the
consumer group members?

Following the approach of throttling, KIP-1010 proposes to introduce
partition-level quotas, which may provide a solution to this issue.

> Do you think we got enough to move forward on this as a KIP?

The KIP process is open to everyone, encouraging community members to share
their ideas and proposals in details. Alternatively, wrapping the consumer
to implement extra functionality is a viable approach. However,
incorporating such functionality into the upstream codebase typically
requires a strong justification.

For another, kafka connector is a good example which wraps the consumer to
be paused/resumed on demand.

Thanks,
Chia-Ping

