Hi Greg:

Thank you very much for the review=2E
Let's do more compares with tiered storage=2E
I agree that the chunk has certain functional overlap with tiered storage=
=2E But they are designed from a different perspective=2E They both want to=
 address the problem that overtime the partition data is too big and hard t=
o manage=2E
The tiered storage design principal is to make the data become somebody el=
se's problem=2E Data can be provided over an interface=2E As long as the in=
terface is implemented properly the external data can be accessed seamlessl=
y=2E
The chunk design principal is to extends current Kafka data plane's capabi=
lity to be able to handle large partition data, fully inside Kafka=2E No ot=
her systems are needed=2E
The main benefit is to manage the data fully in side Kafka data plane in a=
 uniformed way=2E So that there a not too much logic need to be added and t=
he data can be scaled linearly, with the cost of metadata complexity (thank=
s for the KRaft work to make this a lot easier)=2E
Arguably we could implement a default RemoteStorageManager inside Kafka us=
ing a separated disk system=2E But 1) as the name indicated it meant to be =
an external / remote system, and 2) if it is running inside the same JVM wi=
th Kafka certain resources (throughput specifically) need to coordinated wi=
th Kafka broker, this increase complexity=2E And 3) would the data managed =
the same way as partition data or not (e=2Eg=2E has segments and indices)? =
If the same why worth the hassle to add this additional RemoteStorageManage=
r layer? If not why the same partition data managed differently inside the =
same broker?
To clarify, there is no intention to replace tiered storage and the chunk =
meant to work with it=2E Please see "Automatic chunk deletion" section in t=
he KIP=2E

I hope these answer your concerns=2E
Thanks again for your review!

De Gao


