Hi,

Thanks for all the updates and all the discussions regarding this KIP!

IMO, the KIP does not precisely enough explain the rationale behind the 
proposed upgrade path. That makes the KIP hard to understand. I believe 
the KIP should be a bit more explicit about the reasons. That does not 
mean we need to include all details in the user-facing recommendation in 
the docs. But the details are required to agree on the KIP from a 
maintainer perspective.

In other words, more details for agreement on the KIP, but not all 
details are needed for the user-facing recommendation.

BC1:
In the following, I will focus on Kafka Streams, but maybe some 
statements can also be generalized for clients.

In the KIP I miss the statement that deprecated Java APIs are not 
removed for at least three minor releases and can only be removed in the 
next major release after the three minor releases. That information plus 
the fact that the 1.x series only comprised 2 minor releases explains 
why an upgrade from Kafka Streams 0.11.x - 2.3.x to 2.8.x should work 
without compilation errors due to removed public Java APIs. That means 
the Streams app can be re-deployed right after the bump to 2.8.x.

Once on 2.8.x, users should clean up their code and replace all 
deprecated Java APIs with the recommended Java APIs. Then they can bump 
the version to 3.9.x and re-deploy. After the clean-up, the compilation 
should work without compilation errors due to removed public Java APIs. 
The reason for that is that since 2.8.x is the last minor release in the 
2.x series, there cannot exist Java APIs that were deprecated after 
2.8.x and removed in 3.x due to the deprecation rules mentioned above.

Upgrades from 0.11.x - 2.3.x directly to 3.9.x would also work. However, 
some deprecated APIs from 0.11.x - 2.3.x (or even 2.4.x or 2.5.x) might 
have been already removed from 3.9.x. One can fix that, but the 3.9.x 
javadocs do not contain the deprecation notice anymore and it is harder 
to understand what replaces the deprecated API. It seems to be smoother 
to use the bridge release.

If we follow this strategy, we can also consider not treating EOSv1/v2 
explicitly as Sophie proposed, because the upgrade from EOSv1 to EOSv2 
would happen automatically during the code clean-up.
However, I am not sure whether that also applies to eager vs. 
cooperative. Maybe we have to call out the upgrade from eager to 
cooperative explicitly.

To sum up, the upgrade path would be easier understandable if it the KIP 
explained the implications with the deprecation rules.


BC2:
Another aspect that confuses me in the KIP is the bridge version for 
clients. If my broker version is 3.9.x, I can do a direct upgrade from 
1.0.x to 4.0.x (let apart the Java API compatibility mentioned above). 
The 4.0.x release tests 4.0.x clients against 3.9.x brokers and the 
3.9.x release tests 1.0.x clients against 3.9.x brokers. It is not clear 
to me why we need a bridge release in this case.

The situation changes if the brokers are at 4.0.x.

It seems for clients the table mixes broker compatibility with Java API 
compatibility. It would be clearer if the KIP stated what broker 
versions is assumed in the table.

In general, users need to upgrade clients and Streams to at least 2.1 to 
use them with 4.0.x brokers. The KIP states that in the notes below the 
table. I believe, this statement should go above the table in a more 
prominent location.


Best,
Bruno

p.s.: Many thanks to Matthias for the long discussion that led to this 
long e-mail.



