On 2025/02/28 07:35:10 "Matthias J. Sax" wrote:
> Thanks all. I am happy that we are making progress to close this out.
>=20
> As mentioned in my (long) email last Friday, I also think that the KIP=20
> makes a lot of implicit assumptions, and it might benefit from begin=20
> more explicit, even explaining things that are "set in stone" already,=20
> and the KIP does not change them (like API deprecation timelines and=20
> guarantees). But as Bruno said, it would help a lot to make it easier to=20
> understand the KIP.
>=20
> Only a very small number of people seems to fully understand all the=20
> nuances, and it took quite a while to collect and curate all the cases,=20
> to shape this KIP... And we know that a lot of people actually read=20
> KIPs, and the title for KIP-1124 is or sure click-bait...
>=20
> I am also happy to edit the wiki page directly if that's ok with Kuan,=20
> to add background information that I believe is missing and important.
>=20
>=20
>=20
> My overall understanding of the state of the KIP, ie, its actual content=20
> seems to be, that there is a high level agreement, and the content of=20
> the KIP is ok.
>=20
> The only open question seems to be, if we want to recommend `2.8.x` as a=20
> bridge release for Kafka Streams or not. As said in my email on Friday,=20
> I am personally ok either way, with a preference to add `2.8.x` as=20
> bridge release, as it simplifies updating the code by avoiding=20
> compilation errors. With a 2.8.x bridge release, KS application code=20
> would _always_ compile, and the JavaDocs help a lot to guide users how=20
> to migrate off deprecated APIs. -- With a direct upgrade from older=20
> versions to 3.9.x, code does not compile, and there is no guidance as=20
> the old methods (and corresponding JavaDocs) are already removed, making=20
> it significant harder for user to update their code and make it compile=20
> again.
>=20
> In the end, we would only _recommend_ 2.8.x as a bridge release to=20
> users, and there won't be any claim that an direct upgrade to 3.9.x is=20
> not supported. If one wants to do this, it's tested, and ok. Knock=20
> yourself out -- there is for sure apps out there, that are just a simple=20
> `builder.stream(...).filter(...).to(...)` the there won't be an API=20
> incompatibility issues.
>=20
> But I don't think it would be wise to _recommend_ a direct upgrade to=20
> 3.9.x for the reasons stated, as many apps are more complex.
>=20
> Thoughts?
>=20
>=20
>=20
> About testing: I did look into the system tests, and it seems we=20
> actually don't really have a real upgrade test for clients, ie, a test=20
> that is running an consumer or producer with some older versions, and=20
> executes a rolling bounce to upgrade the app...
>=20
> But maybe such a test is not necessary for clients (compare to Kafka=20
> Streams), and the existing client-broker forward/backward compatibility=20
> test are good enough? (At least in the last 10 years, it seems this test=20
> coverage was sufficient?)
>=20
> I want to point out though that I agree with Bruno: If we assume that=20
> the client-broker compatibility tests are sufficient to verify client=20
> upgrades, we do effectively/implicitly test upgrading 2.0 (and older=20
> clients) to 4.0 using 3.9 and older brokers. These test coverage does=20
> not exist in a single branch so, but test from different branches=20
> combined, implicitly cover this. Nevertheless, is it tested.
>=20
> So it seems both Bruno and Ismael are right, depending how you look at=20
> the problem. In any case, even if we test such an upgrade implicitly, I=20
> agree that it's good to exclude these upgrades officially as unsupported.
>=20
>=20
>=20
> -Matthias
>=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> On 2/27/25 6:10 AM, Ismael Juma wrote:
